---
// The tag cloud under the carousel on the home page. Added 2026-09-22 at the owner's request,
// from a page he gave as the reference: telo.by/o_sayte/, which prints a heading over a cloud of
// multi-coloured words. Three things about it were changed on purpose, and each is his decision:
//
//   no heading  - the reference writes "QUICK NAVIGATION" above its cloud; this one stands alone.
//   one colour  - the reference paints every word a different colour. Here the size alone says
//                 which tag carries the most entries, and the colour is the site's blue at one
//                 shade, so the block reads as one thing rather than as confetti.
//   tags only   - every word here is a taxonomy term with a page behind it.
//
// WHY THIS IS HTML AND NOT A CANVAS
//
// The reference draws its cloud into a canvas with a word-cloud library. That would cost a
// dependency, and it would cost the three properties this block exists for: the words are links
// to /tags/<slug>/, a search engine has to see them, and a screen reader has to read them. A
// canvas has none of the three, and the reference shows the other price of it - its own words are
// clipped at the edge of the bitmap, because the layout does not know how wide the canvas is.
// Anchors in a wrapping flex row give the same silhouette, cost nothing and cannot be clipped.
//
// WHY THE SIZE IS LOGARITHMIC
//
// The counts on this site run from 33 entries down to 1, so a size proportional to the count
// would print one word many times the height of another and the rest as dust. The logarithm maps
// whatever range the content has onto the seven steps below.
//
// WHY IT IS HIDDEN ON A PHONE, AND WHY AT THAT WIDTH
//
// The owner asked for a cloud "on the desktop, from a certain width up", and for it to disappear
// exactly where the hamburger appears. The header replaces its menu with a hamburger below 640px
// (the toggle in Header.astro carries sm:hidden), so the cloud is hidden below 640px and shown
// from there up. If that breakpoint in Header.astro moves - there is a note there proposing 768px
// - this one moves with it, because the rule the owner gave is "no cloud while the menu is a
// hamburger", not "no cloud below 640px".
//
// The row of round menu links on the home page is the other half of that rule and carries the
// mirror image of these classes: the two swap at the same width and are never on screen together
// (src/pages/index.astro). Below the breakpoint the header menu is a hamburger, so the page needs
// navigation of its own and the round links are it; above it the header shows every menu item, so
// the links would only repeat the header and the cloud takes the space instead.
//
// It is a CSS media query rather than an Astro directive. client:media hydrates a framework
// component, and this project has no framework component at all; Astro's own template directives
// reference adds that when an element is already shown and hidden by CSS, the directive is the
// harder way round. So the markup is in the page on a phone too, unseen - which is what lets a
// crawler read the links whatever the viewport is.
//
// HOW IT MOVES
//
// Asked for by the owner on 2026-09-22, in three parts, and each part is here for a different
// reason. The words breathe - every one of them drifts a few pixels up and down on a slow loop of
// its own length and its own phase, so the block never settles into a pulse. A word grows under
// the pointer while every other word rolls back - smaller, dimmer - so that the cloud has one
// word at a time in front. And the cloud turns like a globe, taking its speed and its direction
// from where the pointer is over it: that is src/scripts/tag-cloud.ts, which is also where the
// reference for all of this is written down. The owner pointed at telo.by, whose cloud is jQuery
// TagCanvas, and asked for its behaviour without the colours and without the outline box it
// draws around the word under the cursor. A fade-in on load was offered and he did not want it.
//
// What is rendered here is the wrapping row, and the globe is a class the script adds. So the
// markup a crawler or a screen reader reads is a plain list of links in a fixed order, which is
// exactly what the reference throws away by drawing into a canvas: on that page the links sit in
// a list marked display:none beside the picture.
//
// Both the breathing and the globe stop for a visitor who has asked for less motion, in the media
// query at the foot of the stylesheet here and in the matching test in the script. Nothing is
// lost but the movement: the cloud is a wrapping row of the same links either way.
//
// The motion values are derived from the name of the tag, not from Math.random(). A random value
// would be drawn again on every build, so two builds of unchanged content would differ and the
// screenshot comparison this project relies on would report a change that nobody made.
import { site } from "../config";
import { getTerms } from "../lib/terms";
import { encodeSlug } from "../lib/urlize";

// The same source as /tags/ and the tag pages, so a word here cannot point at a page that does
// not exist: getTerms() has already dropped drafts and future dates. It runs at build time, in
// component frontmatter, through top-level await (Astro 4, "Data Fetching"), so a visitor
// receives finished HTML.
//
// The local binding widens the readonly tuple that `as const` gives site.tagCloud.hidden into a
// readonly string[]. Without it, includes() would only accept the three literal names and reject
// any other string, which is the opposite of what a filter needs.
const hiddenTags: readonly string[] = site.tagCloud.hidden;
const tags = (await getTerms("tags")).filter(
  (tag) => !hiddenTags.includes(tag.name),
);

// Smallest first. Written out as literals because Tailwind scans the source for class names and
// generates only the ones it can see; an assembled name would come out unstyled.
//
// The scale starts at text-base and not at text-sm since 2026-09-22, at the owner's request: a
// tag with one entry was 14px, and on the globe the far side of the sphere shrinks a word further
// still, which left the rarest tags as a target too small to put a pointer on. The floor of that
// depth scale was raised in src/scripts/tag-cloud.ts at the same time, and the anchors carry a
// little padding, so the area that answers a click is larger than the letters in it.
const sizes = [
  "text-base",
  "text-lg",
  "text-xl",
  "text-2xl",
  "text-3xl",
  "text-4xl",
];

const counts = tags.map((tag) => tag.cards.length);
const least = Math.log(Math.min(...counts));
const most = Math.log(Math.max(...counts));

/** Which of the size steps a tag with this many entries gets, smallest 0. */
const stepOf = (count: number): number =>
  // One below the largest when every tag is used equally often, which is also a site with a
  // single tag: there is no range to spread over, and the largest size for everything would shout.
  most === least
    ? sizes.length - 2
    : Math.round(
        ((Math.log(count) - least) / (most - least)) * (sizes.length - 1),
      );

// A small number that is always the same for the same word. Thirty-one and a prime modulus are
// the usual pair for a string hash; nothing here needs it to be a good one, only to be stable and
// to differ between neighbouring words.
const hashOf = (text: string): number => {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let index = 0; index < text.length; index += 1) {
    sum = (sum * 31 + text.charCodeAt(index)) % 9973;
  }
  return sum;
};

/**
 * How one word moves: how far it drifts, how long one breath takes, and where in that breath it
 * starts. Written twice into the markup, and that is deliberate - the same three numbers drive
 * the wrapping row in CSS and the sphere in the script, so the block looks the same either way.
 *
 * As custom properties they feed the keyframes at the foot of this file. As data attributes they
 * are read once by src/scripts/tag-cloud.ts, which folds the breathing into the position it
 * computes, because in sphere mode the script owns the transform and a CSS animation on the same
 * property would win over it and freeze the word in place.
 *
 * The phase is a negative delay, which starts an animation part-way through instead of waiting -
 * without it every word would begin its first breath together and the cloud would pulse once
 * before falling out of step. The depth is the odd one out: it belongs to the row alone, where it
 * stands the larger words in front, while the sphere gets its depth from where a word is on it.
 */
type Motion = { drift: number; breath: number; phase: number; style: string };

const motionOf = (name: string, step: number): Motion => {
  const seed = hashOf(name);
  const drift = 3 + (seed % 4);
  const breath = 6 + (seed % 5);
  const phase = (seed % 60) / 10;
  const depth = (step - (sizes.length - 1) / 2) * 12;
  return {
    drift,
    breath,
    phase,
    style: `--drift:${drift}px;--breath:${breath}s;--phase:-${phase}s;--depth:${depth}px`,
  };
};
---

{
  /* Nothing to draw before the site has tags, and Math.min of an empty list is Infinity, which
  would put NaN into a class name. The terms arrive sorted by their front-matter name, which is
  what mixes the sizes: alphabetical order has nothing to do with how often a tag is used, so the
  big words fall among the small ones instead of forming a pyramid.

  Two elements and not one: the outer one is what the script measures and the inner one is what
  it moves the words inside of, so that a box read from the page is never a box that is itself
  being transformed.

  This wrapping row is the markup, and the sphere is a class the script adds to it. Everything
  that reads the page rather than looks at it - a crawler, a screen reader, find-in-page, a
  visitor with JavaScript off or with reduced motion asked for - gets this row, which is the same
  set of links in the same order. */
}
{
  tags.length > 0 && (
    <nav
      aria-label="Tags"
      class="tag-cloud mx-auto hidden max-w-3xl px-4 pt-10 sm:block"
    >
      <div class="tag-cloud__sky flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-3">
        {tags.map((tag) => {
          const step = stepOf(tag.cards.length);
          const motion = motionOf(tag.name, step);
          return (
            <span
              class="tag-cloud__word"
              style={motion.style}
              data-drift={String(motion.drift)}
              data-breath={String(motion.breath)}
              data-phase={String(motion.phase)}
            >
              <a
                href={`/tags/${encodeSlug(tag.slug)}/`}
                title={`${tag.title} (${tag.cards.length})`}
                class:list={[
                  "tag-cloud__link font-semibold leading-none text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-500 dark:text-blue-300 dark:hover:text-blue-100",
                  sizes[step],
                ]}
              >
                {tag.title}
              </a>
            </span>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </nav>
  )
}

<script>
  import "../scripts/tag-cloud";
</script>

<style>
  /* Scoped, unlike the carousel's and the search page's stylesheets, and for the reason those two
     are not: every element here is rendered by this component, so Astro's own attribute reaches
     all of them. The script sets two custom properties and creates no markup. */

  /* The rules below come in two sets. The first is the wrapping row, which is what the markup is
     and what a visitor without JavaScript or with reduced motion keeps. The second, under
     .tag-cloud--sphere, is the globe, and that class is added by the script - so nothing here
     assumes the script ran. */

  .tag-cloud {
    /* The depth the row is seen through: it is what makes the per-word translateZ visible at
       all. The sphere does its own projection in the script and does not use this. */
    perspective: 900px;
  }

  .tag-cloud__sky {
    /* Without this the words are flattened into the parent, and their translateZ - the whole
       depth of the row - is discarded. */
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
  }

  .tag-cloud__word {
    display: inline-block;
    transform: translate3d(0, 0, var(--depth, 0px));
    animation: tag-cloud-breathe var(--breath, 8s) ease-in-out var(--phase, 0s)
      infinite;
  }

  /* ---------- the globe ---------- */

  /* A height, because the words leave the flow: absolutely positioned children give their parent
     nothing to be as tall as. clamp keeps it a sphere rather than a letterbox on a wide screen
     and stops it eating the page on a small one. */
  .tag-cloud--sphere {
    position: relative;
    height: clamp(17rem, 34vw, 23rem);
  }

  .tag-cloud--sphere .tag-cloud__sky {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
  }

  /* Every word starts at the middle and the script moves it from there, which is why the inline
     transform it writes begins by pulling the word back by half its own size. The breathing is
     off because the script folds it into that same transform: an animation beats an inline
     declaration on the property it animates, so leaving it on would pin the word to the centre
     and only let it bob. */
  .tag-cloud--sphere .tag-cloud__word {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    animation: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    will-change: transform, opacity;
  }

  /* The transform is written out in full in both frames, because a transform property replaces
     the one on the element rather than adding to it: leaving translateZ out of a frame would drop
     the word to the back of the block for the length of the breath. */
  @keyframes tag-cloud-breathe {
    0%,
    100% {
      transform: translate3d(
        0,
        calc(var(--drift, 4px) * -1),
        var(--depth, 0px)
      );
    }
    50% {
      transform: translate3d(0, var(--drift, 4px), var(--depth, 0px));
    }
  }

  /* The word under the pointer grows and every other word rolls back - smaller, dimmer, further
     away - so that one word at a time is the one being read. Asked for by the owner on
     2026-09-22, after the hover growth was already in.

     It is the `scale` property and not a scale() inside `transform`, and that is the whole reason
     this works: `transform` on the span is owned by the breathing animation, and an animation
     beats an ordinary declaration on the same property, so a transform written here would simply
     never be applied. `scale`, `rotate` and `translate` are separate properties in CSS today and
     compose with whatever `transform` is doing.

     The condition is :has() and not a plain :hover on the container, because the gaps between the
     words belong to the container too: with :hover the whole cloud would recede whenever the
     pointer crossed a gap, with nothing standing out to explain why. A browser without :has()
     ignores both rules and keeps the plain growth below, which is the reason that one is written
     on its own as well. */
  .tag-cloud__link {
    display: inline-block;
    /* Room around the letters, so that the area answering a click is bigger than the word - the
       smallest tags are a few characters wide and the globe shrinks them further on the far side.
       Added 2026-09-22 with the larger smallest size, for the same reason. */
    padding: 0.15rem 0.3rem;
    transition:
      scale 0.25s ease-out,
      opacity 0.25s ease-out,
      color 0.2s ease-out;
  }

  .tag-cloud__link:hover {
    scale: 1.2;
  }

  .tag-cloud__sky:has(.tag-cloud__link:hover) .tag-cloud__link {
    scale: 0.85;
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  .tag-cloud__sky:has(.tag-cloud__link:hover) .tag-cloud__link:hover {
    scale: 1.2;
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Everything that moves by itself is off for a visitor who asked for less motion: the
     breathing here, and the globe in the script, which makes the same test and never adds its
     class - so the block stays the row it is rendered as and simply stands still. The hover
     growth stays, without its transition: it happens only while the pointer is held on a word,
     which is the visitor moving, not the page. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .tag-cloud__word {
      animation: none;
    }

    .tag-cloud__link {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
